From 0b7dd3097ab12d68a9839de13a0cd14a88e59cec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 08:42:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/40] Look at the starting block again when the global
 address walk comes round to it

CheckGlobalAddressSumYPointer starts in the middle of a block, behind an
address computation "reg = global + index", and marked that block as visited
at once.  When the block is in a loop the walk comes back to its top, found
the mark and returned success without looking at the instructions in front
of the computation.  A read through (reg),y there, which the first
iteration reaches with the pointer the loop was entered with, was never
seen: the computation was removed, the read was not patched, and it read
through the index in the low byte and a stale high byte.

    struct obj * otmp = &first;
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        r += otmp->quan;
        otmp = &objs[k];
    }

Mark the block only when it is entered at its top, as
CheckBoolBitPropagation and CheckForwardSumYPointer do, so that the entry
tests (pointer required on entry, loop head, entries dominated by the
starting block) and the walk over its front part apply to it as to any
other block.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 oscar64/NativeCodeGenerator.cpp | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/oscar64/NativeCodeGenerator.cpp b/oscar64/NativeCodeGenerator.cpp
index 4605c4a..527f32d 100644
--- a/oscar64/NativeCodeGenerator.cpp
+++ b/oscar64/NativeCodeGenerator.cpp
@@ -38102,10 +38102,10 @@ bool NativeCodeBasicBlock::CheckGlobalAddressSumYPointer(const NativeCodeBasicBl
 {
 	if (!mPatched)
 	{
-		mPatched = true;
-
 		if (at == 0)
 		{
+			mPatched = true;
+
 			if (!mEntryRequiredRegs[reg] && !mEntryRequiredRegs[reg + 1])
 				return CheckPatchFailUse();
 
